  <h2 id="roadmap">A realistic roadmap if you are not at a top IIM</h2>
  <p>
    Here is what an honest plan looks like when the campus-placement door is not realistically open to you.
    The exact pace depends on your starting point — some people move through the earlier stages in a
    focused stretch of months, others need a couple of years, and both are normal.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick your lane early:</strong> decide between the CA-to-IB lateral, the CFA-plus-portfolio route, or Big 4 transaction advisory based on your current degree, funding, and timeline — not on which sounds most prestigious.</li>
    <li><strong>Start the modelling portfolio immediately, in parallel:</strong> do not wait until you qualify as a CA or clear CFA Level 2 to build your first 3-statement model and DCF. Build it now, on a company you actually understand, and improve it as your finance knowledge deepens.</li>
    <li><strong>Target the on-ramp role deliberately:</strong> inside Big 4, aim specifically for transaction advisory, valuation, or deal advisory teams, not audit-only tracks. Inside a CA articleship, seek out audit clients or projects with real M&A or valuation exposure.</li>
    <li><strong>Set a lateral checkpoint at 2-3 years:</strong> if you are still doing only routine advisory or audit work with no deal exposure by then, actively push for a transfer or start applying externally rather than waiting for the role to evolve on its own.</li>
    <li><strong>Get outside validation before every real application:</strong> show your model and pitch to someone already working in IB and use their feedback to close gaps before a real interview is on the line.</li>
    <li><strong>Build AI-tool fluency as a stated skill, not a hidden one:</strong> be ready to talk in an interview about how you use AI for first-draft modelling or research and how you check it for errors.</li>
  </ul>

